WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.320
Part 3. You will hear two architecture students called Mia and Leo discussing their presentation

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:16.240
on houses of the future. First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 23.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:46.639
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 23.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:53.919
You know that joint presentation we've got to do this semester, Leo?

00:00:53.919 --> 00:00:58.880
On houses of the future? That's right. I'm a bit concerned.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.799
Are we meant to come up with creative new suggestions for these houses?

00:01:02.799 --> 00:01:08.480
I don't think so. It's more a matter of reporting and evaluating possible developments.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.400
But we mustn't be too general. We've got to support our points by referring to specific

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:21.919
cases. So that'll need a lot of work. I'm afraid so. When's it got to be done by?

00:01:21.919 --> 00:01:25.919
In about six weeks. So that's not too much of a rush.

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:30.720
Good. We'd better decide now what type of housing we're going to focus on.

00:01:31.279 --> 00:01:34.879
How about housing for different generations living together?

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:41.279
We could do. Or accommodation for one person? I think someone else is doing that.

00:01:42.239 --> 00:01:47.839
I was wondering about housing for the elderly. That's likely to become more important.

00:01:47.839 --> 00:01:52.400
Yeah, that's true. But I think your suggestion about intergenerational

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.519
living might be more interesting. Let's go with that.

00:01:55.519 --> 00:02:02.559
OK. Now, I think the future demand is mainly going to be for accommodation in urban areas.

00:02:02.559 --> 00:02:08.320
So one way of meeting that demand might be to use existing commercial buildings

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.559
and adapt them to form accommodation.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.960
Or come up with original ways of organising space so that people can live in smaller homes.

00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:21.839
But I think the solution is to design multi-storey apartment blocks.

00:02:21.839 --> 00:02:24.240
Building up rather than out. Yes.

00:02:27.759 --> 00:02:30.479
Before you hear the rest of the discussion,

00:02:30.479 --> 00:02:34.800
you have some time to look at questions 24 to 30.

00:02:51.839 --> 00:03:14.320
Now listen and answer questions 24 to 30.

00:03:15.039 --> 00:03:19.039
Let's think of some specific developments for houses of the future.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:25.199
OK. How about increased use of roof space on high-rise buildings for gardens?

00:03:25.199 --> 00:03:32.399
Yes. In fact, it doesn't have to be high-rise. You can do it on a one-storey building in a suburb.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:37.199
But it would greatly improve how you feel if you live in an urban high-rise.

00:03:37.199 --> 00:03:39.520
Especially if you don't have a balcony.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:45.360
Yes. I think homes of the future will all need access to a shared working space

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.119
somewhere in the same building or group of houses

00:03:49.119 --> 00:03:53.839
where people can go and work instead of just having a laptop on the kitchen table.

00:03:53.839 --> 00:03:58.720
Yes. So they aren't having to travel to an office but can still interact with others.

00:03:59.279 --> 00:04:04.559
That's often how new ideas get generated by chatting to someone from a different profession.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:11.839
Yeah. I read about a type of design where the internal walls of an apartment are moveable

00:04:11.839 --> 00:04:15.919
so the space can be adapted over time as people's needs change.

00:04:15.919 --> 00:04:20.799
Like when children leave school and start working but still continue to live with their parents for

00:04:20.799 --> 00:04:25.679
many years or when an elderly relative moves in with the family. It would mean they could

00:04:25.679 --> 00:04:29.519
still have their own space specially designed for their needs.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:38.320
Yes. Have you heard about those smart bathroom mirrors which can monitor people's health?

00:04:39.119 --> 00:04:43.279
They recognise signs of illness and contact a doctor automatically.

00:04:44.559 --> 00:04:46.239
Not so sure they're a good idea.

00:04:46.799 --> 00:04:50.959
Nor am I. People might worry about conditions which aren't serious at all.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:57.359
What about transport? Wouldn't it be good if there were bike sheds with charging points

00:04:57.359 --> 00:05:02.959
so people could store their electric bikes securely and charge them up at the same time?

00:05:02.959 --> 00:05:07.839
Yes. That would encourage more people to cycle instead of using their car.

00:05:08.559 --> 00:05:09.839
Much better for the planet.

00:05:10.799 --> 00:05:15.679
I read about one housing development where cars had to be left just outside it

00:05:15.679 --> 00:05:20.959
so the centre was all a pedestrianised area. Great for families with children.

00:05:20.959 --> 00:05:25.920
Maybe. But what if you're disabled or elderly and can't walk far?

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.320
It wouldn't be so good for people like that.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:29.119
No.

00:05:29.119 --> 00:05:34.640
I saw a scheme for communal vegetable plots where neighbours could decide what to grow together.

00:05:35.359 --> 00:05:38.559
That'd be a great way for older people to get to know one another,

00:05:39.119 --> 00:05:41.920
especially if they're no longer going out to work.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.760
Yes. Doing something together's always more enjoyable, isn't it?

00:05:46.399 --> 00:05:47.920
Do you think we might be able to look into...

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:59.920
That is the end of part 3. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to part 3.

